Great 🚀 This is a very common interview question for DevOps/SRE/Kubernetes engineers. Let’s break it down simply:

🔹 Why Kubernetes Dropped Docker Runtime (after v1.20)?
Kubernetes did not drop Docker containers ❌ — it only dropped Docker as a runtime.

The key reason:

Kubernetes uses CRI (Container Runtime Interface) to talk to runtimes.

Docker ❌ does not implement CRI directly.

Instead, Docker has its own stack → Docker Engine → containerd → runc.

For Kubernetes to use Docker, it had to run an extra component called dockershim (a “bridge” between K8s and Docker).

This added extra complexity and overhead.

So, Kubernetes maintainers decided:
👉 Remove dockershim and talk directly to containerd or CRI-O (both CRI-compliant).

🔹 Architecture Before & After
Before (with Docker):
Kubernetes (kubelet)
       |
       v
   dockershim (bridge)
       |
       v
    Docker Engine
       |
       v
   containerd
       |
       v
     runc
After (with containerd / CRI-O directly):
Kubernetes (kubelet)
       |
       v
  containerd / CRI-O (CRI-compliant)
       |
       v
     runc
🔹 What does this mean for users?
Your images still work ✅ (Docker images are OCI-compliant, and containerd/CRI-O can pull and run them).

You don’t need Docker runtime on Kubernetes nodes anymore.

If you still want docker build locally → you can still use Docker CLI on your laptop. But inside Kubernetes clusters, runtimes are usually containerd or CRI-O now.

🔹 Real-Life Analogy
Think of Docker as a middleman:

Kubernetes → tells middleman (dockershim) → who tells Docker Engine → who tells containerd → who tells runc.

Too many hops 🛑

Kubernetes dropped the middleman and now talks directly to containerd or CRI-O, making things simpler and faster.

✅ Interview Answer (short & crisp):

Kubernetes dropped Docker runtime after v1.20 because Docker was not CRI-compliant. Kubernetes had to use an extra component called dockershim to talk to Docker, which added complexity. Since Docker internally used containerd anyway, Kubernetes removed dockershim and now directly supports CRI-compliant runtimes like containerd and CRI-O. Docker images still run without issues.

