"If my Kubernetes cluster is using Docker runtime, how do I migrate to containerd or CRI-O?"

Let’s go step by step.

🔹 Why Migration is Needed
Kubernetes deprecated Docker runtime (dockershim) after v1.20.

Clusters running docker:// as runtime must move to containerd or CRI-O.

If not migrated → kubelet won’t start containers.

🔹 Migration Steps (Docker → containerd)
1️⃣ Check Current Runtime
kubectl get node <node-name> -o jsonpath='{.status.nodeInfo.containerRuntimeVersion}'
If it shows docker://20.x → using Docker runtime.

If it shows containerd://1.x → already migrated.

2️⃣ Install containerd on Nodes
On each node (control plane + workers):

sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y containerd
Configure default settings:

sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd
containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml
Restart service:

sudo systemctl restart containerd
sudo systemctl enable containerd
3️⃣ Update kubelet to Use containerd
Edit kubelet service config (/var/lib/kubelet/kubeadm-flags.env or systemd unit):

--container-runtime=remote \
--container-runtime-endpoint=unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock
Reload and restart kubelet:

sudo systemctl daemon-reexec
sudo systemctl restart kubelet
4️⃣ Verify Migration
Check node status again:

kubectl describe node <node-name> | grep -i "Container Runtime"
✅ Should now show:

Container Runtime Version: containerd://1.6.20
🔹 Migration to CRI-O (Alternative to containerd)
Install CRI-O packages (version must match Kubernetes version, e.g., 1.28).

Start and enable CRI-O:

systemctl enable crio --now
Update kubelet config:

--container-runtime=remote \
--container-runtime-endpoint=unix:///var/run/crio/crio.sock
Restart kubelet.

🔹 Notes (Real Time)
Docker images still work ✅ because they follow OCI format.

Developers can still build images with Docker locally → push to registry → run with containerd/CRI-O in cluster.

Migration should be done in staging cluster first, then production.

Most managed Kubernetes services (EKS, GKE, AKS) already use containerd by default → you don’t need to migrate manually.

✅ Interview-Ready One-Liner Answer:

Migration from Docker to containerd/CRI-O involves installing the CRI-compliant runtime (containerd or CRI-O), updating kubelet configuration to point to the new runtime socket, and restarting kubelet. After migration, Kubernetes nodes will show containerd:// or cri-o:// instead of docker://. Docker images remain compatible since they follow the OCI standard.

